Found 2026-08-31 while verifying that the B056 canonicalization fix could not
move a diagnostic. It cannot - but the reason turned out to be broader than
expected, and worth its own ticket.

## Actual Behavior

`openplanet-lsp check` is SILENT on all of these:

```angelscript
void Mismatch() {
    // two different engine enums
    EDummyCollectionIdent a = EDummyCollectionIdent::Undefined;
    EGhostNameLogoType b = a;              // silent

    // two workspace-declared enums
    LocalOne c = LocalOne::X;
    LocalTwo d = c;                        // silent
}
enum LocalOne { X }
enum LocalTwo { Y }
```

Zero diagnostics. The file IS being analysed - a positive control in the same
file (`Meta::ZZZNoSuchEnumName` as a type, and a wrong-arity call) is flagged
normally, so this is not a parse or resolution failure.

Also silent, and probably the same root: a bad enum VALUE.

```angelscript
EDummyCollectionIdent a = EDummyCollectionIdent::NoSuchValueZZZ;   // silent
```

## Expected Behavior

UNVERIFIED - the game was down when this was found, so there is no live
compiler verdict here. AngelScript does implicitly convert enum values to
integer types, so it is genuinely possible that some or all of the above are
LEGAL and the LSP is correct to stay silent. That must be probed before any
fix. Specifically worth probing separately:

- enum A -> enum B assignment (the case above)
- enum -> int / uint assignment (likely legal)
- int -> enum assignment (likely requires a cast)
- an undeclared value on a known enum (`E::NoSuchValue`)

A game REJECT on any of these makes it a real missing diagnostic. A game
ACCEPT closes that row and is worth recording either way, since right now the
behaviour is simply unknown.

## Why this was found

B056's canonicalization fix changes `canonicalize_type_name` for 57 enums.
`named_types_equivalent` (`src/typecheck/checker.rs`) compares canonicalized
names, so the fix looked like it could move an argument-type diagnostic. It
cannot - measured on a purpose-built plugin covering locals, parameters,
return types, arrays and both scope qualifiers, base vs branch binaries, zero
difference. The reason is that no enum-mismatch diagnostic exists to move.

That makes the B056 fix safe on stronger grounds than the call-graph argument
originally given for it, and it means `named_types_equivalent`'s enum handling
is currently reachable only for unique EXTERNAL overload sets.

## Related

B056 (source names / canonicalization), B023 (function-shaped args), GH #47
(`No matching signatures` render).

## Independently reproduced, and the ticket SPLITS (coordinator, 2026-08-31)

Reproduced on a master-based release binary
(`~/.cache/lsp-openplanet-builds/b041-base/release/openplanet-lsp`), separate
from the lane's measurement. Positive controls in the same file fire normally —
`error: unknown type ZZZNoSuchType` and
`error: function Format expects 2 args, got 0` — while every enum row stays
silent. **The null result is real, not the checker failing to look.**

**A fifth row, and it does not belong with the other four.** An undeclared
value on a KNOWN enum is silent for BOTH workspace-declared and engine enums:

```angelscript
enum LocalOne { X }
LocalOne a = LocalOne::ZZZNotAValue;          // silent  (workspace enum)
auto b = EDummyCollectionIdent::ZZZNotAValue; // silent  (engine enum)
LocalOne c = LocalOne::X;                     // silent  (correct)
auto d = EDummyCollectionIdent::Undefined;    // silent  (correct)
```

So the LSP never diagnoses a non-existent enum MEMBER at all.

**Why that splits this ticket:**

- **Rows 1-3 (enum -> other enum, enum -> int, int -> enum) genuinely need the
  game.** AngelScript implicitly converts enums to integers, so silence may
  well be correct. Do not guess; probe when the game is back.
- **The undeclared-member row does NOT need the game to be actionable in the
  workspace case.** `enum LocalOne { X }` is declared in the same file the LSP
  is analysing; it has complete information and does not need the engine to
  know `ZZZNotAValue` was never declared. No implicit-conversion story can make
  a member that does not exist into a legal one — implicit conversion is about
  the TYPE of a value, and here there is no value.

Confidence on the workspace case is high but not absolute, so still probe it
for completeness when the game returns — it is one row, and this repo's history
says a spelling that "obviously" must be rejected is worth ten seconds of
compiler time. But it is reasonable to implement and test the workspace half
without waiting, and that is the recommended split.

The engine-enum half is closer to B052/B056 territory (which spellings the
engine accepts) and should wait for a probe.


## The gap is specific to ENUM members (2026-08-31)

Measured on one plugin, branch binary. Every other kind of undeclared
qualified member IS diagnosed; only enum members are silent.

```angelscript
enum LocalOne { X }
class LocalCls { int Field; }
namespace LocalNs { void Fn() {} }

LocalOne a = LocalOne::ZZZNotAValue;          // SILENT   <-- the gap
auto d = EDummyCollectionIdent::ZZZNotAValue; // SILENT   <-- the gap
LocalNs::ZZZNotAFunction();                   // error: No matching symbol 'LocalNs::ZZZNotAFunction'
c.ZZZNotAField = 1;                           // error: type `LocalCls` has no member `ZZZNotAField`
ZZZNotAnything();                             // error: undefined identifier `ZZZNotAnything`
```

So this is not a broad "member checking is missing" gap. Three sibling
paths - namespace member, class member, free identifier - already produce
exactly the right diagnostic. Enum-value resolution is the one path that
resolves the ENUM and then accepts any member name without checking it
against the declared values.

That makes the workspace half narrow and low-risk to fix: the value list is
already on `EnumInfo`, and there is a well-formed message shape to copy from
the namespace case.

Note the split confirmed by the coordinator: the WORKSPACE case
(`enum LocalOne { X }` declared in the analysed file) needs no game probe -
the LSP has complete information and implicit enum-to-int conversion is about
the type of a value, and here there is no value. The ENGINE case and rows 1-3
above still want a live verdict.

## A sibling instance, same sweep (lane-b057, 2026-08-31)

Walking the `named_types_equivalent` call sites turned up a second silent case
in this family: **assigning to an engine PROPERTY does not flag an enum
mismatch either.** Identical on both binaries, so pre-existing and not caused by
the B056 follow-up.

That extends this ticket's population beyond locals and parameters. When the
game is back, probe the property-assignment form alongside the four rows
already listed — if enum→enum is legal by implicit conversion, all of these
close together; if it is not, this is another site needing the same diagnostic.

See also B076 (a funcdef handle accepts a function whose RETURN type differs) —
found in the same grep sweep, same "type equivalence is not fully checked"
family, but a distinct defect.

## GAME PROBED (coordinator, 2026-08-31) — four of five rows are real, one row CLOSES

The game came back up. Every row in this ticket now has a live compiler
verdict. Each fixture was compiled as its OWN unit (never batched — B068).
Controls ran in the same session: a must-be-clean fixture returned CLEAN and a
must-error fixture returned `Identifier 'ZZZNoSuchType' is not a data type`,
so the oracle discriminates in both directions.

| row | game verdict | engine message |
|---|---|---|
| enum A -> enum B (engine) | REJECT | `Can't implicitly convert from 'EDummyCollectionIdent' to 'EGhostNameLogoType'.` |
| enum A -> enum B (workspace) | REJECT | `Can't implicitly convert from 'LocalOne' to 'LocalTwo'.` |
| enum -> int (workspace) | CLEAN | — |
| enum -> int (engine) | CLEAN | — |
| int -> enum (engine) | REJECT | `Can't implicitly convert from 'int' to 'VirtualKey'.` |
| bad member, engine enum | REJECT | `No matching symbol 'VirtualKey::ZZZNotAValue'` |
| bad member, workspace enum | REJECT | `No matching symbol 'LocalOne::ZZZNotAValue'` |

**ROW 2 CLOSES. `enum -> int` is LEGAL and the LSP's silence is CORRECT.**
Do not implement a diagnostic for it. The ticket suspected this; it is now
measured rather than assumed.

**The other four rows are genuine missing diagnostics.** The implicit-conversion
story that made row 2 legal does NOT extend to them — the engine rejects
enum->enum in both directions of provenance, rejects int->enum, and rejects a
member that was never declared on either an engine or a workspace enum.

**A confound in the first pass, and how it was cleared — read this before
trusting the row-2 result.** My first fixtures used `EDummyCollectionIdent::Undefined`,
which DOES NOT EXIST in the live game: rows 1, 2 and 4 all carried a stray
`No matching symbol 'EDummyCollectionIdent::Undefined'`. That does not disturb
the REJECT rows — an error appeared, and an error is self-evidencing. It is
fatal for row 2, whose conclusion is drawn from the ABSENCE of an error, which
is exactly the direction that fails silently.

Row 2 was therefore re-measured three ways:

1. `r2s`: `VirtualKey a = VirtualKey::A;` alone -> CLEAN. This is the
   anti-vacuity guard: it proves the fixture reaches the typechecker at all,
   so a later CLEAN means 'accepted', not 'never looked'.
2. `r2w`: workspace enum -> int, zero engine dependency -> CLEAN.
3. `r2e`: verified-real engine enum -> int -> CLEAN.

`VirtualKey::A` was taken from `docs/probes/b053-bare-enum-values.tsv`, where
it is already recorded as a live-game ACCEPT — a known-good value rather than
a guessed one. Rows 3 and 4 were also re-run against `VirtualKey` so that no
surviving conclusion rests on the bad-value fixture.

**Use the engine's own wording for the new diagnostics.** `Can't implicitly
convert from 'A' to 'B'.` and `No matching symbol 'E::V'` — matching the
engine's text is what lets a user searching an error string find both tools.

**The recommended split in the section above still stands and is now stronger.**
The workspace undeclared-member case needs no engine knowledge and can be
implemented first; the game has now confirmed the engine agrees, so that half
carries no residual risk.

Probe fixtures were staged outside the repo; `issue_repro_game.py` unloads and
removes each one (leave-as-found). Nothing was added to tests/fixtures/.

## Corpus committed

The verdicts above, plus the invalid row and both anti-vacuity controls, are
committed as `docs/probes/b071-enum-conversion.tsv` (master @ 58416a4).

The `lsp_today` column there is measured, not inherited: a release binary
built from a586825 (a verified ancestor of master), run as
`openplanet-lsp check` in each fixture directory. Every mismatch row exits
rc=0 with no error. Two signals in the same run show that is real silence and
not an unanalysed file — the negative control returned rc=1
`unknown type \`ZZZNoSuchType\``, and the two workspace-enum rows emitted
`an enum value ... is never used` warnings.

So the implementer starts from: five confirmed missing diagnostics, one row
closed as correct-silence, the engine's exact wording for each message, and a
fixture set that is already known to be non-vacuous.

## GAME PROBE — ground truth, measured 2026-08-31 (lane-b060-b062)

**This supersedes the ticket's UNVERIFIED expectation. Write the fix against
this table, not against the prose above.** Ten cases, each its OWN compilation
unit (never batched — B068), controls in their own units, run against the
engine and then the same ten through the LSP at master `a17a991`.

| case | ENGINE | LSP | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ctl-pos` trivially valid | ACCEPT | ACCEPT | both harnesses sound |
| `ctl-neg` unknown type | REJECT | REJECT | both harnesses sound |
| `engine-reachable` `EGhostNameLogoType::None` | ACCEPT | ACCEPT | premise holds |
| `engine-cross` two engine enums | **REJECT** | ACCEPT | **MISSING** |
| `workspace-cross` two workspace enums | **REJECT** | ACCEPT | **MISSING** |
| `bad-value-workspace` | **REJECT** | ACCEPT | **MISSING** |
| `bad-value-engine` | **REJECT** | ACCEPT | **MISSING** |
| `enum-to-int` `int i = LocalOne::X` | ACCEPT | ACCEPT | **MUST STAY SILENT** |
| `int-to-enum` `LocalOne e = 5` | **REJECT** | ACCEPT | **MISSING — not in this ticket** |
| `enum-arith` `LocalOne::X + LocalTwo::Y` | ACCEPT | ACCEPT | **MUST STAY SILENT** |

Engine messages, verbatim:

    Can't implicitly convert from 'EDummyCollectionIdent' to 'EGhostNameLogoType'.
    Can't implicitly convert from 'LocalOne' to 'LocalTwo'.
    No matching symbol 'LocalOne::NoSuchValueZZZ'
    Can't implicitly convert from 'int' to 'LocalOne'.

All five REJECTs are the trustworthy direction — an ACCEPT on this machine can
leak from another installed plugin, a REJECT cannot. The two ACCEPTs that
matter (`enum-to-int`, `enum-arith`) are sound anyway: nothing in those units
resolves through a foreign namespace and the enums are declared in the fixture.
Both controls behaved on both sides, so neither harness was silently broken.

## Three things the probe changes

**1. The surface is larger than this ticket states.** `int -> enum` is rejected
by the engine and silent in the LSP, and is not mentioned above. **The
asymmetry is the whole point: enum -> int is ACCEPTED, int -> enum is
REJECTED.** A fix written as "enums and ints are interchangeable" gets one of
them backwards whichever way it guesses — structurally the same trap as B061's
crossed pair.

**2. Two boundaries a naive fix would break.** `enum-to-int` and cross-enum
ARITHMETIC are both accepted by the engine and **must stay silent**; two
different enum types in one arithmetic expression is fine because both decay to
int. So the fix scopes to **assignment/initialisation compatibility**, NOT to
"two enum types appearing in one expression". Without those two pinned as
required-silent, a plausible implementation ships false errors on code the game
compiles.

**3. This ticket's own repro line is wrong and would have misled the
implementer.** It uses `EDummyCollectionIdent::Undefined`; the typedb says that
enum's only value is `Dummy`. So that line fails for **bad value**, not for
**cross-enum assignment** — two different defects, both on this ticket's list,
and anyone reproducing it verbatim would have got a REJECT and attributed it to
the wrong cause. The probe used `EDummyCollectionIdent::Dummy` to isolate the
cross-enum question.

Also checked because everything else depended on it: both engine enums are
**member-scoped** in the dump (nested under `CGameGhost`), yet `engine-reachable`
shows they are usable as bare global type names.

## ACCEPTANCE CRITERION (coordinator, supersedes the Test plan above)

**Six required diagnostics** — the four originally claimed, plus `int -> enum`,
plus the engine bad-value case — **and two required silences**: `enum -> int`
and cross-enum arithmetic.

The silences are the over-firing gate and **must live in the same fixture as
the errors**, so "just flag any enum type mismatch" cannot pass. Same
construction as `TrulyDead` in B062.

## Implementation warning (lane-b064, from B076)

**Do not simply extend `check_init_conversion`.** It is called from **exactly
one** of the four initializer positions — `check_var_decl_local` only. Not
globals, not class fields, not assignments. That asymmetry is exactly what B076
turned out to be: a correct check reached from one position while three others
were silently unchecked. A fix landing there covers local variables only and
the identical bug class reappears one ticket later.

Prefer a sibling method with the same signature, called from all four
positions; a shared dispatcher is a THIRD step once both behaviours are pinned,
not something folded into either fix.

Second trap from the same lane: a regression test that runs with
`type_index: None` has no engine enum to resolve, so it asserts silence and
gets silence **from a code path that never executed**. `EDummyCollectionIdent`
only exists with a typedb loaded. Load the fixture typedb and add an
anti-vacuity control proving the engine enum IS live.